<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    LITTLE CARE — PHASE 14
    Feed Baby configuration.

    This file is an EXTERNAL appSettings file. It is loaded by Web.config via
        <appSettings file="Web.FeedingVideo.config">
    which is the same pattern the project already uses for
    Web.MonitoringMedia.config, so deployment-specific values can be changed
    without editing (and re-versioning) the main Web.config.

    RENAME THIS FILE to "Web.FeedingVideo.config" in the deployed site to
    activate the feature. A committed ".example" is NOT read at runtime, so the
    feature is safely DISABLED until an operator copies it into place.

    ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    STORAGE ROOT
    ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    The directory that holds every feeding video. It MUST be OUTSIDE the web
    root so the folder is never served statically: the only way to read a file
    is then through the authorized API endpoint, which re-checks the caller's
    entitlement on every request.

    The server writes <root>\<JobId>\<ChildId>\<Guid>.webm and NOTHING else. No
    name, job id, child id, token or other personal data ever reaches a
    filename - the name is a GUID generated by the server.

    IMPORTANT: the IIS application pool identity needs WRITE access to this
    folder. Grant it explicitly; do not widen permissions on the drive.

    FAIL CLOSED: if this key is missing or blank, the feature is disabled and
    the API reports that storage is unavailable. It never falls back to a path
    inside the application folder.
-->
<appSettings>
    <add key="FeedingVideoStorageRoot" value="E:\feeding ababy video" />

    <!--
        Hard cap on recording length, in seconds. The feature brief fixes this
        at 30. The value may TIGHTEN it but is clamped so configuration can
        never raise it beyond 30.
    -->
    <add key="FeedingVideoMaxDurationSeconds" value="30" />

    <!--
        Hard cap on an accepted upload, in bytes. 12 MB is generous headroom for
        a 30-second 360p WebM and a real ceiling: the server aborts the copy
        mid-stream once this is exceeded, so the limit bounds actual disk usage
        rather than being checked after the file has landed.
    -->
    <add key="FeedingVideoMaxFileSizeBytes" value="12582912" />
</appSettings>
